1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,299
When talking about automation and the unstoppable rise of industrial robots,

2
00:00:04,299 --> 00:00:08,580
the image that immediately comes to mind is that of silicon microchips

3
00:00:08,580 --> 00:00:12,400
or perhaps the most recent discoveries in artificial intelligence.

4
00:00:12,400 --> 00:00:15,500
It is quite common to believe that progress happens simply

5
00:00:15,500 --> 00:00:20,539
because technology itself is becoming smarter and faster day by day.

6
00:00:20,539 --> 00:00:24,780
Well, in this analysis we will take a step back and completely change our perspective.

7
00:00:24,780 --> 00:00:28,179
In fact, we will explore a fascinating economic thesis, to say the least,

8
00:00:28,179 --> 00:00:32,320
presented by researchers Daron Acemoglu and Pascual Restrepo.

9
00:00:32,320 --> 00:00:36,179
The central idea of their research is that driving the actual invasion

10
00:00:36,179 --> 00:00:40,880
of robots in factories worldwide is not just pure and simple computing power,

11
00:00:40,880 --> 00:00:44,719
but something much, much more human: demographics.

12
00:00:44,719 --> 00:00:48,000
That's right, the way human populations evolve

13
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:52,960
could be the true invisible engine of automation, leading us to a fundamental question:

14
00:00:52,960 --> 00:00:55,320
will aging bring us robots?

15
00:00:55,340 --> 00:00:59,460
This begs the question: what really drives industrial automation?

16
00:00:59,460 --> 00:01:03,899
If we take a look at today's global landscape, a real mystery emerges.

17
00:01:03,899 --> 00:01:07,460
There are some nations that are filling their production lines

18
00:01:07,460 --> 00:01:12,260
with super-advanced robotic arms at a simply mind-boggling speed.

19
00:01:12,260 --> 00:01:16,900
Other nations, however, despite having exactly the same access to the same technologies,

20
00:01:16,900 --> 00:01:19,980
are moving at a much, much slower pace.

21
00:01:19,980 --> 00:01:23,140
Now, if technology were the one and only driving factor,

22
00:01:23,140 --> 00:01:28,060
adoption should be practically uniform wherever there is capital to invest, right?

23
00:01:28,060 --> 00:01:31,420
And yet, the data shoves massive discrepancies in our faces.

24
00:01:31,420 --> 00:01:35,159
It is precisely this stark disparity that drives us to dig deeper,

25
00:01:35,159 --> 00:01:39,319
to look for structural and deep-rooted causes in local societies and economies.

26
00:01:39,319 --> 00:01:43,599
Let's lay our cards on the table and compare the two main visions.

27
00:01:43,599 --> 00:01:47,799
On one hand, we have the classical thesis, the textbook one,

28
00:01:47,799 --> 00:01:52,439
according to which it is simply the supply of new technologies that drives automation:

29
00:01:52,439 --> 00:01:58,299
a better, more efficient robot is invented, so companies buy it, end of story.

30
00:01:58,299 --> 00:02:03,679
On the other hand, we find the disruptive thesis of the research by Acemoglu and Restrepo.

31
00:02:03,679 --> 00:02:06,480
They literally flip the perspective:

32
00:02:06,480 --> 00:12:12,360
demographic change creates an urgent and desperate demand for automation.

33
00:02:12,360 --> 00:02:17,099
In other words, robots are not adopted just because they are there on display, nice and ready,

34
00:02:17,099 --> 00:02:21,439
but because the age structure of workers is undergoing a radical change,

35
00:02:21,439 --> 00:02:25,579
leaving gaps that only machines can fill efficiently.

36
00:02:25,579 --> 00:02:28,939
To delve into the mechanisms of this economic model,

37
00:02:28,939 --> 00:02:33,139
we must first precisely define the pawns on the chessboard.

38
00:02:33,139 --> 00:02:38,379
In this context, the labor market is divided into two very distinct macro-categories.

39
00:02:38,379 --> 00:02:44,520
First, we have middle-aged workers, who fall into a range from 21 to 55 years old.

40
00:02:44,520 --> 00:02:48,819
Historically, this is the group that possesses the physical vigor and agility required

41
00:02:48,819 --> 00:02:53,699
to perform complex and highly repetitive manual tasks inside factories.

42
00:02:53,699 --> 00:02:58,780
Then we find older workers, meaning everyone over the age of 56.

43
00:02:58,780 --> 00:03:02,060
At that age, statistically and physiologically,

44
00:03:02,060 --> 00:03:06,379
there is a tendency to shift toward support, management, or service roles,

45
00:03:06,379 --> 00:03:10,019
drastically reducing presence in purely manual activities

46
00:03:10,019 --> 00:03:12,379
and, let's face it, physically wearing ones.

47
00:03:12,419 --> 00:03:19,019
So, how does all this transform into a progressive and inexorable economic mechanism?

48
00:03:19,019 --> 00:03:23,099
We can break the process down into three highly logical phases.

49
00:03:23,099 --> 00:03:27,259
Step number one: when an entire population begins to age,

50
00:03:27,259 --> 00:03:33,219
the pool of middle-aged workers, those famous 21 to 55-year-olds, shrinks.

51
00:03:33,219 --> 00:03:37,699
And this creates a severe labor shortage for strictly manual tasks.

52
00:03:37,699 --> 00:03:40,939
Step number two: the most basic laws of economics

53
00:03:40,939 --> 00:03:45,099
teach us that when something is in short supply, its price skyrockets;

54
00:03:45,099 --> 00:03:49,460
consequently, the wages demanded for these specific industrial workers

55
00:03:49,460 --> 00:03:53,419
increase significantly, and here we are at step number three.

56
00:03:53,419 --> 00:03:57,419
This surge in labor costs backs companies into a corner,

57
00:03:57,419 --> 00:03:59,620
forcing them to look for alternatives.

58
00:03:59,620 --> 00:04:03,900
Suddenly, the huge initial investment required to purchase

59
00:04:03,900 --> 00:04:07,900
and install a robot is no longer just a move to look modern,

60
00:04:07,900 --> 00:04:11,340
it becomes not just cost-effective, but absolutely vital,

61
00:04:11,340 --> 00:04:13,420
to avoid going out of business.

62
00:04:13,420 --> 00:04:16,259
Observing the trend of the economic model,

63
00:04:16,259 --> 00:04:20,459
it is very clear how aging inevitably pushes up

64
00:04:20,459 --> 00:04:23,540
the equilibrium wage for middle-aged workers.

65
00:04:23,540 --> 00:04:27,420
The point where demand and supply meet shifts

66
00:04:27,420 --> 00:04:32,939
progressively, step by step, toward an ever-higher labor cost level.

67
00:04:32,939 --> 00:04:35,540
There is a precise mathematical threshold

68
00:04:35,660 --> 00:04:39,700
beyond which the cost of a human worker well exceeds the fixed cost

69
00:04:39,700 --> 00:04:43,580
of leasing, depreciation, and maintenance of a machine.

70
00:04:43,580 --> 00:04:46,939
Well, when demographic dynamics push wages

71
00:04:46,939 --> 00:04:49,379
beyond that red line of no return,

72
00:04:49,379 --> 00:04:51,780
the spark of automation is ignited.

73
00:04:51,780 --> 00:04:54,100
It has nothing to do with a fascination for high-tech,

74
00:04:54,100 --> 00:04:57,140
it is a matter of pure and simple financial survival.

75
00:04:57,140 --> 00:05:01,060
All very logical, of course; the theory is undoubtedly elegant,

76
00:05:01,100 --> 00:05:05,939
but as always in economics, it must pass the relentless test of facts.

77
00:05:05,939 --> 00:05:09,540
Shifting focus to the global data on robot adoption,

78
00:05:09,540 --> 00:05:12,259
if aging is indeed the main driver,

79
00:05:12,259 --> 00:05:15,019
we should find an unequivocal fact.

80
00:05:15,019 --> 00:05:17,139
The countries that age the fastest

81
00:05:17,139 --> 00:05:21,420
should be the exact same ones installing the most industrial robots.

82
00:05:21,420 --> 00:05:22,819
And guess what?

83
00:05:22,819 --> 00:05:25,860
That is exactly what researchers went to measure,

84
00:05:25,860 --> 00:05:29,060
crossing the official demographic data of the United Nations

85
00:05:29,060 --> 00:05:32,819
with the rigorous records of the International Federation of Robotics.

86
00:05:32,819 --> 00:05:38,060
Analyzing the numbers, a very clear divergence among nations immediately stands out.

87
00:05:38,060 --> 00:05:41,259
On one hand, we observe the proportion of older workers

88
00:05:41,259 --> 00:05:43,100
relative to middle-aged ones.

89
00:05:43,100 --> 00:05:45,459
If we turn the spotlight on nations like Germany,

90
00:05:45,459 --> 00:05:47,100
Japan, and South Korea,

91
00:05:47,100 --> 00:05:51,340
their demographic curves point sharply upward, like rockets.

92
00:05:51,340 --> 00:05:53,740
Shifting focus to the number of operational robots

93
00:05:53,740 --> 00:05:55,860
per thousand industrial workers,

94
00:05:55,860 --> 00:05:59,819
we see an almost identical surge for those very same countries.

95
00:05:59,819 --> 00:06:03,060
The United States, on the contrary, shows a significantly slower aging rate

96
00:06:03,060 --> 00:06:05,259
of the workforce,

97
00:06:05,259 --> 00:06:08,980
and in parallel, their robot adoption curve sits there,

98
00:06:08,980 --> 00:06:11,620
much flatter and closer to the general average.

99
00:06:11,620 --> 00:06:12,860
A coincidence?

100
00:06:12,860 --> 00:06:14,340
Absolutely not.

101
00:06:14,340 --> 00:06:16,699
And this brings us to the crucial point,

102
00:06:16,699 --> 00:06:21,220
which can be summarized in a single, powerful figure: 35%.

103
00:06:21,220 --> 00:06:22,980
According to the researchers' estimates,

104
00:06:22,980 --> 00:06:25,620
population aging on its own

105
00:06:25,620 --> 00:06:29,019
explains a whopping 35% of all variations

106
00:06:29,019 --> 00:06:31,300
in robot adoption across different countries.

107
00:06:31,300 --> 00:06:34,699
This is a massive percentage for a single macroeconomic variable.

108
00:06:34,699 --> 00:06:36,699
To give a concrete idea of the scale,

109
00:06:36,699 --> 00:06:40,060
if the United States had experienced the exact same demographic trends

110
00:06:40,060 --> 00:06:42,259
as Germany over the last few decades,

111
00:06:42,259 --> 00:06:46,819
today the gap in robot adoption between the two nations would be practically halved.

112
00:06:46,819 --> 00:06:51,980
The impact of the demographic curve is something tangible, measurable, and massive.

113
00:06:51,980 --> 00:06:53,939
To solidify this entire concept,

114
00:06:53,939 --> 00:06:56,379
one only needs to cross-reference the extent of aging

115
00:06:56,379 --> 00:06:59,300
with the annual increase in robot adoption:

116
00:06:59,300 --> 00:07:03,139
the resulting positive trend line is unequivocal

117
00:07:03,139 --> 00:07:05,019
and leaves little room for doubt.

118
00:07:05,019 --> 00:07:09,259
This is clearly visible both when analyzing the sample on a global scale

119
00:07:09,259 --> 00:07:11,339
and when narrowing the scope of investigation

120
00:07:11,339 --> 00:07:13,659
to OECD industrialized countries alone.

121
00:07:13,659 --> 00:07:18,060
The upper quadrants are always, inevitably, dominated by the usual suspects,

122
00:07:18,060 --> 00:07:20,339
South Korea and Germany first and foremost.

123
00:07:20,339 --> 00:07:22,180
The golden rule is simple:

124
00:07:22,180 --> 00:07:24,500
the more a national workforce ages,

125
00:07:24,500 --> 00:07:29,259
the more robots are called in to keep assembly lines operating.

126
00:07:29,259 --> 00:07:32,459
But watch out, because the story doesn't end

127
00:07:32,459 --> 00:07:36,340
with the simple installation of machinery purchased from who knows where.

128
00:07:36,340 --> 00:07:40,379
Such a profound and seismic shift in the economic fabric

129
00:07:40,379 --> 00:07:44,139
generates inevitable cascade effects on innovation itself.

130
00:07:44,139 --> 00:07:47,579
If there is an absolute need to replace manual labor,

131
00:07:47,579 --> 00:07:50,980
a frantic race is triggered not just to acquire robots,

132
00:07:50,980 --> 00:07:52,900
but to invent them from scratch.

133
00:07:52,900 --> 00:07:58,060
Let us therefore delve into the fascinating connection between a country's demographic structure,

134
00:07:58,060 --> 00:08:00,379
international patent filings,

135
00:08:00,379 --> 00:08:02,980
and exports of cutting-edge technology.

136
00:08:02,980 --> 00:08:07,660
Comparing demographic aging not only with the passive purchase of robots,

137
00:08:07,660 --> 00:08:10,420
but also with the active creation of new patents,

138
00:08:10,420 --> 00:08:13,060
reveals a truly formidable parallel.

139
00:08:13,060 --> 00:08:17,060
On one hand, we have the now well-known correlation between age and purchases.

140
00:08:17,180 --> 00:08:21,459
On the other hand, analyzing patents specifically related to automation

141
00:08:21,459 --> 00:08:24,579
granted to inventors in those same nations,

142
00:08:24,579 --> 00:08:27,699
the trend is incredibly identical.

143
00:08:27,699 --> 00:08:31,620
Countries aging at record rates, like Japan and Germany,

144
00:08:31,620 --> 00:08:35,659
are not content with just being the best clients of the global robotics industry;

145
00:08:35,659 --> 00:08:37,820
they have become its founding fathers.

146
00:08:37,820 --> 00:08:41,500
They are the ones who invent, develop, and patent the systems that they will then

147
00:08:41,500 --> 00:08:43,659
sell to the rest of the world.

148
00:08:43,659 --> 00:08:48,139
At this point, a legitimate question might arise spontaneously:

149
00:08:48,139 --> 00:08:52,299
is it possible that these nations are simply becoming

150
00:08:52,299 --> 00:08:56,139
more technologically advanced in every single field?

151
00:08:56,139 --> 00:08:59,899
The answer supported by the data is a resounding no.

152
00:08:59,899 --> 00:09:01,819
And this is a crucial detail.

153
00:09:01,819 --> 00:09:05,179
The demographic push creates a targeted and exclusive boom

154
00:09:05,179 --> 00:09:09,059
in the export and invention of robotic technologies.

155
00:09:09,059 --> 00:09:12,779
We are talking about a surgical focus on industrial automation:

156
00:09:12,779 --> 00:09:17,100
CNC machines, automatic welders, mechanical arms.

157
00:09:17,100 --> 00:09:21,100
On the other hand, absolutely no effect caused by demographics is found

158
00:09:21,100 --> 00:09:24,980
on the invention of general computers, smartphones, or technology

159
00:09:24,980 --> 00:09:27,059
designed for the final consumer.

160
00:09:27,059 --> 00:09:31,059
Looking specifically at the different classes of patents filed,

161
00:09:31,059 --> 00:09:34,259
the uniqueness of this phenomenon emerges in all its clarity.

162
00:09:34,259 --> 00:09:36,819
There is a genuine explosion of patents closely linked

163
00:09:36,819 --> 00:09:40,059
to robotics, in response to aging.

164
00:10:40,100 --> 00:10:43,500
However, if we go on to analyze other technological classes,

165
00:10:43,500 --> 00:10:47,700
this strong correlation vanishes into thin air or even turns negative.

166
00:10:47,700 --> 00:10:51,780
Categories such as computers, software, nanotechnology, or even pharmaceutical

167
00:10:51,780 --> 00:10:54,220
products do not register the same boost

168
00:10:54,220 --> 00:10:57,980
stemming from the shortage of workers aged 21 to 55.

169
00:10:57,980 --> 00:11:01,220
In short, the shortage of hands in the factory stimulates the mind

170
00:11:01,220 --> 00:11:03,260
to invent new mechanical arms,

171
00:11:03,260 --> 00:11:05,980
certainly not new accounting software.

172
00:11:05,980 --> 00:11:08,100
Proceeding with this in-depth analysis,

173
00:11:08,100 --> 00:11:10,180
we come to the heart of the matter:

174
00:11:10,180 --> 00:11:14,740
the macroeconomic effects of this immense transformation.

175
00:11:14,740 --> 00:11:18,060
Once robots cross the factory gates

176
00:11:18,060 --> 00:11:21,340
to compensate for the chronic shortage of young workers,

177
00:11:21,340 --> 00:11:24,980
what happens to the total productivity of that country?

178
00:11:24,980 --> 00:11:28,540
And what happens to the share of wealth destined for human labor?

179
00:11:28,540 --> 00:11:32,180
In theory, demographic aging, taken in isolation,

180
00:11:32,180 --> 00:11:34,259
would tend to depress productivity,

181
00:11:34,259 --> 00:11:37,779
simply because there are fewer working-age people.

182
00:11:37,779 --> 00:11:40,539
But this is precisely where automation comes into play,

183
00:11:40,539 --> 00:11:44,019
acting as a powerful counterweight to stem

184
00:11:44,019 --> 00:11:46,179
and reverse this decline.

185
00:11:46,179 --> 00:11:48,579
The words used by the authors of the research

186
00:11:48,579 --> 00:11:51,220
to summarize it all are perfect.

187
00:11:51,220 --> 00:11:53,860
Demographic change encourages the adoption

188
00:11:53,860 --> 00:11:56,100
and development of automation technologies.

189
00:11:56,100 --> 00:11:59,740
This single statement crystallizes a fundamental concept.

190
00:11:59,740 --> 00:11:01,939
Automation is not an abstract enemy;

191
00:11:01,939 --> 00:11:04,419
it acts as a genuine direct substitute

192
00:11:04,419 --> 00:11:07,459
for production workers, the classic blue-collar workers,

193
00:11:07,500 --> 00:11:10,900
at the exact moment they cross the threshold of 56 years old

194
00:11:10,900 --> 00:11:14,340
and begin to gradually exit the heaviest manual tasks.

195
00:11:14,340 --> 00:11:16,860
The economy is a living organism that adapts,

196
00:11:16,860 --> 00:11:19,379
finding ways to ensure that production lines

197
00:11:19,379 --> 00:11:20,740
never stop.

198
00:11:20,740 --> 00:11:23,740
Of course, observing the distribution of the phenomenon

199
00:11:23,740 --> 00:11:25,540
across various industrial sectors,

200
00:11:25,540 --> 00:11:28,700
it is clear that the effect is not the same for everyone.

201
00:11:28,700 --> 00:11:30,860
There are industries such as chemicals,

202
00:11:30,860 --> 00:11:34,180
plastics, or heavy machinery production

203
00:11:34,180 --> 00:11:36,620
that historically rely heavily

204
00:11:36,620 --> 00:11:38,299
on middle-aged workers.

205
00:11:38,299 --> 00:11:41,259
And it is completely logical that these very sectors

206
00:11:41,259 --> 00:11:44,139
experience the strongest backlash from aging,

207
00:11:44,139 --> 00:11:48,500
consequently recording robot adoption at a frantic pace.

208
00:11:48,500 --> 00:11:50,539
On the contrary, sectors like textiles,

209
00:11:50,539 --> 00:11:53,539
which depend far less on that specific age group

210
00:11:53,539 --> 00:11:55,980
and often require complex manipulation

211
00:11:55,980 --> 00:11:58,620
that is difficult to automate 100%,

212
00:11:58,620 --> 00:12:02,220
show extremely slower rates of adoption.

213
00:12:02,220 --> 00:12:04,740
To clear up any doubt and confirm

214
00:12:04,740 --> 00:12:07,139
that we are not just talking about cultural differences

215
00:12:07,139 --> 00:12:08,539
between distant nations,

216
00:12:08,539 --> 00:12:11,539
the researchers zoomed in within the borders

217
00:12:11,539 --> 00:12:14,019
of a single country: the United States.

218
00:12:14,019 --> 00:12:16,580
Well, even at a purely local level,

219
00:12:16,580 --> 00:12:19,620
examining the various counties and commuting zones,

220
00:12:19,620 --> 00:12:22,500
the upward trend line is very clear.

221
00:12:22,500 --> 00:12:24,659
American areas where the active population

222
00:12:24,659 --> 00:12:26,580
is aging most rapidly

223
00:12:26,580 --> 00:12:28,820
are turning into real magnets

224
00:12:28,820 --> 00:12:31,139
for so-called robot integrators—

225
00:12:31,139 --> 00:12:33,060
that is, companies specialized

226
00:12:33,060 --> 00:12:36,580
in selling, installing, and maintaining industrial automation.

227
00:12:36,580 --> 00:12:39,300
That global demographic effect replicates itself,

228
00:12:39,300 --> 00:12:42,100
like a fractal, on a micro local scale.

229
00:12:42,100 --> 00:12:43,460
Incredible, right?

230
00:12:43,460 --> 00:12:47,220
All of this guides us toward an inescapable conclusion

231
00:12:47,220 --> 00:12:49,940
and toward a question that projects this analysis

232
00:12:49,940 --> 00:12:52,020
directly into our future.

233
00:12:52,020 --> 00:12:55,060
Knowing for certain that the demographic curves of much

234
00:12:55,060 --> 00:12:56,980
of the West and the Far East

235
00:12:56,980 --> 00:13:00,340
are inexorably oriented toward rapid aging,

236
00:13:00,340 --> 00:13:02,980
how will demographic policies of the coming decades

237
00:13:02,980 --> 00:13:05,779
influence the technology market?

238
00:13:05,779 --> 00:13:07,860
The era of automation we are entering

239
00:13:07,860 --> 00:13:09,779
with giant strides is not dictated

240
00:13:09,779 --> 00:13:12,259
exclusively by the geniuses of Silicon Valley,

241
00:13:12,259 --> 00:13:14,500
but is paced by the relentless,

242
00:13:14,500 --> 00:13:17,300
slow ticking of the global demographic clock.

243
00:13:17,300 --> 00:13:19,779
If the world population continues to age,

244
00:13:19,779 --> 00:13:21,379
the ultimate rise of robots

245
00:13:21,379 --> 00:13:24,019
is not just a likely sci-fi scenario,

246
00:13:24,019 --> 00:13:26,100
it is an absolute economic necessity

247
00:13:26,100 --> 00:13:27,860
that we will not be able to escape.